<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1816">1816</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of the Two Sicilies was the largest state of pre-unification <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, formed in 1816 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king Ferdinand I merged the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. It covered the southern mainland and Sicily, with Naples as its capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> overthrew it in 1860, and it was annexed to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Two Sicilies" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Flopao; derivative work by ZuppaDiCarlo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_the_Two_Sicilies_(1816)_(Sodacan_style).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Two Sicilies goes back to the partition of the medieval Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Norman warriors conquered the island from the Saracens, who had ruled it since 827, and in 1131 Roger II made the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and the island a Kingdom of Sicily. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a> (1282 to 1302) the island passed to the Crown of Aragon, but the rulers on the mainland, though known unofficially as the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, never gave up the title King of Sicily. Both realms were thus called the Kingdom of Sicily, and so their reunion was named the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies.</p><p>In 1516 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> fell to Charles V. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, the peace treaties of 1713/14 left Naples with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and gave Sicily to Victor Amadeus of Savoy, who exchanged it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1720 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Both were ruled by Austria until 1735. In 1734 Spanish troops supporting the claims of the Spanish Infante Charles had won at Bitonto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Treaty of Casalanza restored Ferdinand IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and returned the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> to him. In 1816 he annulled the Sicilian constitution of 1812, Sicily was fully reintegrated and the state became the Kingdom of the Two Sicilies, with Ferdinand as Ferdinand I. Some deeds of the administration of Kings Joseph and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a>, such as reforms to the civil, penal and commercial codes, were kept and extended to Sicily.</p><p>During 1848 and 1849 the kingdom suppressed the revolution and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>'s attempt at secession with its own forces, including hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-guards">Swiss Guards</a>. The Army of the Two Sicilies had also put down the Calabrian revolution of 1847, but it collapsed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> in 1860. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> overthrew the king, and a plebiscite voted to join the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. The annexation completed the first phase of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> was proclaimed in 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The kingdom held about 36 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s population around 1850. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> was the largest city in Italy and the third largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Land was concentrated in the hands of a few families, and villages housed a large, poor rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>. The kingdom had the largest merchant fleet in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Its achievements include the first steamboat in the Mediterranean in 1818, the first railway on the Italian peninsula in 1839, connecting Naples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portici">Portici</a>, and the first volcano observatory, the Osservatorio Vesuviano, in 1841.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of the Two Sicilies” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Italy grew out of the Risorgimento, the movement to unite a peninsula divided among Austrian provinces, the Papal States, the Bourbon <b>Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</b> and several duchies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By a law of 8 December 1816 he merged his two realms into the <b>Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</b>, taking the title Ferdinand I, and the separate Kingdom of Naples ceased to exist.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ferdinand IV also ruled Naples, twice took refuge on the island during the French wars; after his restoration in Naples he merged the two realms by laws of December 1816 into the <b>Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</b>, abolishing the Sicilian constitution and parliament and ending the separate kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1820 a regiment of the army of the <b>Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</b>, commanded by the Carbonaro Guglielmo Pepe, mutinied and King Ferdinand I agreed to a constitution.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
